  "debu1": {
   "front": "B N RI Textbook of Arts for Grade 4 Prelims.indd 1 29-03-2025 18:01:08 Reprint 2026-27",
   "chapters": [
    {
     "file": "debu101",
     "pages": 17,
     "head": "1 The Panchatantra is a set of stories written in the Sanskrit language more than two thousand years ago. It was written by a well-known Sanskrit scholar, Vishnu Sharma. He wrote this collection to teach valuable lessons on morals and values to everyone, especially children, in a very friendly way. The word Pancha means five and Tantra means codes or principles. All the stories in the Panchatantra are categorised into five groups. These stories are so popular that they have been read by children across the world for centuries. These stories have been translated into all Indian languages and many foreign languages, such as English, German, Italian, Arabic, Japanese, Spanish, and many others. Here is one such interesting story from the Panchatantra— PANCHATANTRA Visual Arts.indd 1Visual Arts.indd 1 29-03-2025 5.24.19 PM29-03-2025 5.24.19 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu102",
     "pages": 8,
     "head": "Chapter 2 TEXTURES IN NATURE Many stories from the Panchatantra, Hitopadesha, and Jataka tales are set in jungles. Nature is full of incredible shapes, forms, colours, patterns, and textures. Textures can be felt through touch–rough, smooth, bumpy, soft, and everything in between. In this chapter, you will step outside to explore the textures in your natural surroundings. Then you will create your own Kalpavriksha or wish-fulfilling tree by learning techniques like: • Frottage—rubbing on textured surfaces to capture their patterns. • Collage—cutting and pasting pieces of paper, leaf, etc., on a surface. • Leaf printing—painting the surface of a leaf and pressing it on a surface. Visual Arts.indd 18Visual Arts.indd 18 29-03-2025 5.24.37 PM29-03-2025 5.24.37 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu103",
     "pages": 11,
     "head": "Chapter 3 AQUA WORLD Aqua is another word for water. Aquarium starts with this word. Aqua is also used to describe some shades of colour, such as aqua blue or aqua green. Dive into this chapter and let your imagination flow. Observe patterns as you draw, colour, and create many aquatic creatures. Twist and turn lines into animal forms, as you create new designs of your own. Construct hanging artworks called mobiles, that move. What are you waiting for? Put on your art goggles and dive right in with a wondrous splash! Visual Arts.indd 26Visual Arts.indd 26 22-Apr-25 10:52:03 AM22-Apr-25 10:52:03 AM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu104",
     "pages": 12,
     "head": "Chapter 4 PEOPLE IN ACTION Recall a time when you were in a crowded place with people moving around in different positions and actions. Each person’s posture and movement could tell a different story. Nandalal Bose’s paintings in Chapter 1 show a variety of postures and actions of people. In this chapter, you will learn to use geometric shapes to sketch people in action. Inspired by traditional Indian toys, you will craft geometric 3D forms to make a spinning doll and enjoy playing with it. Get ready to imagine, create, and have fun! Visual Arts.indd 37Visual Arts.indd 37 29-03-2025 5.24.49 PM29-03-2025 5.24.49 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu105",
     "pages": 12,
     "head": "THEATRE 2 Theatre.indd 492 Theatre.indd 49 29-03-2025 5.30.37 PM29-03-2025 5.30.37 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu106",
     "pages": 12,
     "head": "Imagination is a very powerful tool. If you are able to imagine with great detail, it is like having a superpower — You can come up with new ideas, tell new stories and even solve problems! Do you want to know how? Here is an interesting story related to Arjun. IMAGINE Chapter 6 Around 500 years ago, in Chitrapur, a grand play about the Mahabharata war was being performed. Villagers had gathered to see it. The play began … When it was the most exciting part where Arjuna had to enter the stage, the actor playing the role stumbled backstage. His shiny costume tore on a sharp edge. The colourful fabric was ruined. Everybody was tense and anxious. Without the costume, how could Arjuna continue? 2 Theatre.indd 612 Theatre.indd 61 29-03-2025 5.30.48 PM29-03-2025 5.30.48 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu107",
     "pages": 9,
     "head": "Have you tried walking like an old person with a walking stick? Or you must have tried to stand or sit like your mother holding a handbag. You also might have tried imitating an animal in the way it walks, jumps, or sniffs. Isn’t it fun to behave like someone else? Did you know that this is a very important technique in theatre! It is called IMITATION. Imitation in theatre is a foundational technique where actors observe and replicate the actions, voices, or facial expressions of others to create believable characters. It requires an in-depth study of real-life people, animals, or even objects and bringing their qualities to life on stage. Let us play a simple game to understand how it feels to imitate—seriously. LET’S CREATE Chapter 7 2 Theatre.indd 732 Theatre.indd 73 29-03-2025 5.31.06 PM29-03-2025 5.31.06 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu108",
     "pages": 9,
     "head": "There are always many ideas and inspirations you can get from things around you. You first start with the people around you. The variety of situations and characters can help you create many interesting stories. Next, you may look at animals. Their behaviour and actions are quite interesting to adapt to stories and performances, as you have already tried in Chapter 5. Is there an animal that you feel very connected to? What characteristics of the animal do you like? How would you want to create a story about it if you had to? LOOK AROUND Chapter 8 Now, let us look beyond humans and animals. What do we have? Beautiful nature! Trees, rivers, mountains, blowing winds … do they not make you feel happy and peaceful? 2 Theatre.indd 822 Theatre.indd 82 03-Oct-25 10:02:28 AM03-Oct-25 10:02:28 AM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu109",
     "pages": 9,
     "head": "MUSIC Music.indd 91Music.indd 91 29-03-2025 2.38.58 PM29-03-2025 2.38.58 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu110",
     "pages": 8,
     "head": "• Indian classical music is a very ancient tradition that started from the Vedas. • Later, it developed into two main styles — Carnatic, or South Indian Classical Music, and Hindustani or North Indian Classical Music. • Indian Classical Music is generally passed down through oral tradition where the student (shishya) would spend many years with their guru, developing a very special bond. Chapter 10 MAKING MUSIC Music.indd 100Music.indd 100 29-03-2025 2.39.20 PM29-03-2025 2.39.20 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu111",
     "pages": 7,
     "head": "A music genre is a category that groups music based on shared characteristics, sounds, or patterns. Can you name some genres of music that you have heard? What is unique about them? We can generally categorise music into four major categories: 1. Folk music — Lok sangeet 2. Classical music — Hindustani and Carnatic 3. Other traditional forms — Devotional music, Rabindra Sangeet, semi-classical, etc. 4. Popular or creative music — Film songs, pop songs, etc. Chapter 11 SONGS AND STORIES Hindustani classical, Baul gaan, bollywood songs, Hip hop — you may have heard some of these styles of music. Different kinds of music sound different. They have unique characteristics of style, patterns, origin, instruments and purpose. Music.indd 108Music.indd 108 29-03-2025 2.39.30 PM29-03-2025 2.39.30 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu112",
     "pages": 12,
     "head": "That is the emotive power of music. Go online and find a scene from one of your favourite movies. Press the mute button and watch the scene without sound. Now watch the same scene with sound. How did it feel in each of the cases? Share your thoughts and feelings with the class. Crash! Boom! Sssshhhh! Dishoom! Hisss! Can you imagine a scary movie or a superhero movie scene without sound effects? It would not feel the same, right? Music and sound effects can significantly add to the emotion and drama of a story. They can set the mood of a scene and add intensity to the dialogue and action. They can build tension and make you feel like you are on the edge of your seat. They can also make you feel relaxed and happy. Chapter 12 THINK, FEEL, CREATE Music.indd 115Music.indd 115 03-Oct-25 2:08:27 PM03-Oct-25 2:08:27 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
    {
     "file": "debu113",
     "pages": 10,
     "head": "In Grade 3, we learnt how to create rhythm patterns using claps, snaps, and stamps. Now, let us learn how else we can use our body to create percussion sounds and rhythm patterns. Then, let us explore the sounds of musical instruments. How do we use our body language and gestures to express how we feel? Chapter 13 SOUNDS AND INSTRUMENTS Learn this activity and then see if you can add a few more actions of your own. STOMP SNAP CLAPS Music.indd 127Music.indd 127 29-03-2025 2.39.58 PM29-03-2025 2.39.58 PM Reprint 2026-27"
    },
